{"name":"Silver Chalice","hex":"#acaea9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"},{"term":"chalice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large drinking cup, often having a stem and base and used especially for formal occasions and religious ceremonies.","A kind of water-cooled pipe for smoking cannabis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chalice"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Silver Chalice (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"The Silver Chalice is a 1952 English language historical novel by Thomas B. Costain. It is the fictional story of the making of a silver chalice to hold the Holy Grail and includes 1st century biblical and historical figures: Luke, Joseph of Arimathea, Simon Magus and his companion Helena, and the apostle Peter."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/82/The_Silver_Chalice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Silver_Chalice_(novel)","wikidata":"Q3821163"},{"title":"The Silver Chalice (film)","blocks":[{"text":"The Silver Chalice is a 1954 American epic historical drama film directed and produced by Victor Saville, based on Thomas B. Costain's 1952 novel of the same name. It was one of Saville's last films and marked the feature film debut of Paul Newman; despite being nominated for a Golden Globe Award for his performance, Newman later called it \"the worst motion picture produced during the 1950s.\""}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/40/Silver_Chalice_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Silver_Chalice_(film)","wikidata":"Q1999028"},{"title":"Chalice","blocks":[{"text":"A chalice is a drinking cup raised on a stem with a foot or base. Although it is a technical archaeological term, in modern parlance the word is now used almost exclusively for the cups used in Christian liturgy as part of a service of the Eucharist, such as a Catholic mass. These are normally made of metal, but neither the shape nor the material is a requirement. Most have no handles, and in recent centuries the cup at the top has usually been a simple flared shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/German_-_Chalice_with_Saints_and_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_Walters_44116.jpg/330px-German_-_Chalice_with_Saints_and_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_Walters_44116.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalice","wikidata":"Q210723"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Parts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png/500px-Parts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Parts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png/500px-Parts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diagram showing the parts of a liturgical chalice.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Agape_feast_03.jpg/500px-Agape_feast_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Agape_feast_03.jpg/500px-Agape_feast_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fresco of a female figure holding a chalice at an early Christian Agape feast. Catacomb of Saints Marcellinus and Peter, Via Labicana, Rome","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAgape_feast_03.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/PotirMuzeuCJ.JPG/500px-PotirMuzeuCJ.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/PotirMuzeuCJ.JPG/1280px-PotirMuzeuCJ.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/PotirMuzeuCJ.JPG/500px-PotirMuzeuCJ.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver chalice in the museum of the Romanian Orthodox Archbishopy of the Vad, Feleac, and Cluj","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APotirMuzeuCJ.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Chalice_Burgos_VandA_132-1873.jpg/500px-Chalice_Burgos_VandA_132-1873.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Chalice_Burgos_VandA_132-1873.jpg/1280px-Chalice_Burgos_VandA_132-1873.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb